A||||10||CJ||88 Brake Job||||1||
Z||000000||CJ||06-05-2001||10:59 AM||||Need to replace the pads on all four wheels. <P>The shop manual shows that you do not have to remove the caliper (great, don't want to do that if I can avoid it).  They say to remove the spring pins with a special slide hammer, pulling them away from the vehicle center.  Then the pads slide out, and a special tool is used to rotate the pistons into the caliper.<P>Has anyone changed the rear pads without removing the caliper, without the special tools?  I have not found the slide hammer with the fittings needed for the spring pins.  I found a piston rotator tool, but it clearly is too thick to get in there with the caliper still installed.<P>Thanks...<P>Chris<BR>Novi, MI<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000001||Raydar||06-05-2001||11:05 AM||||Chris, <BR>I drove my pins out with a drill bit that was slightly smaller than the pin. You HAVE to drive them out from behind. <BR>I don't know about rotating the piston with the rear caliper still on the car, as I had a leaky piston seal. Replaced both rear calipers.<BR>BTW... Welcome to the Forum!<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 06-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||ChadMan||06-05-2001||11:31 AM||||I used a drill bit too.  You can't use anything much smaller than the pin or you'll "flare" the end of the pin, locking it in place...the pin looks like it's made of rolled-up sheetmetal.<P>The "slide hammer" Haynes calls for doesn't seem to exist...I couldn't find one anywhere.<P>I was able to seat the piston without removing the caliper by GENTLY tapping in the recessed area on the face of the piston with a small hammer and punch, turning the piston a few millimeters at a time.  You don't want to tap very hard or you'll risk jamming the piston in its bore.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Songman||06-05-2001||12:22 PM||||But why not remove the caliper? Removing the caliper is easy. The tough part is getting the piston back in and I can imagine it would be much tougher with the caliper still in place... It took me half an hour even with the caliper off and using the right tool! The slot is just too dang shallow.<P>To do it right you need to turn your rotors anyway.... Do yourself a favor, remove the caliper. You don't have to unhook it from the brake line or anything, just unmount it so you can work with it...<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||lawrence||06-05-2001||12:53 PM||||I made myself a tool to remove the pins.  I got some 6-32 threaded rod and some 6-32 nuts.  It's a one-time tool, but once I got the spring pins out, I replaced them with 1/4" bolts.<P>I submitted a write-up to the Online Service Guide, so if you can find it, all the info is there.<P>Good luck,<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe/sts" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe/sts</A>  - Sequential Turn Signals<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe/products" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe/products</A>  - WebElectric Products||||reg||1
Z||000005||Will||06-05-2001||01:11 PM||||I made some SS pins, hled in place with hairpin clips to replace the stock roll pins.<P>The caliper is easiest to work with if you unbolt it, remove the rotor, then reinstall the caliper.||||reg||1
Z||000006||pHoOl||06-05-2001||09:36 PM||||*bump*<P>Hi dad!!<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who can't outrun the cops.<P>AIM: phool88fiero<P>    <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/pHoOl_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||mrfiero||06-06-2001||12:47 PM||||You DON'T have to remove the roll pins!!!  Don't waste your time with it......the pads will come off without removing them.  The '88 calipers are 2-piece.......the main caliper body and the outer part.  All you need to do is remove the 2 bolts that hold the outer part to the main body and pop it off.  You may need to use a screwdriver to pry it off of the rollpins, but it will come off.  Next, remove the outboard pad and remove the rotor (you need to resurface it anyways).  Now you can remove the inbaord pad.  That's it!  I have never removed the rollpins on any of my '88 Fieros (and I've owned about 15).<P>Good luck.<P>  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||84Bill||06-06-2001||03:04 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mrfiero:<BR><B>You DON'T have to remove the roll pins!!!  Don't waste your time with it......the pads will come off without removing them.  The '88 calipers are 2-piece.......the main caliper body and the outer part.  All you need to do is remove the 2 bolts that hold the outer part to the main body and pop it off.  You may need to use a screwdriver to pry it off of the rollpins, but it will come off.  Next, remove the outboard pad and remove the rotor (you need to resurface it anyways).  Now you can remove the inbaord pad.  That's it!  I have never removed the rollpins on any of my '88 Fieros (and I've owned about 15).<P>Good luck.<P>  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thats exactly what I do BUT! it is not recommended in any of the manuals to split the caliper halvs for some reason, so I don't recommend it either since it involves brakes and hence liability.<BR>Do this at your own risk! <P>||||reg||4
Z||000009||PJ||06-06-2001||05:37 PM||||The quickest and easiest way to pull the pins is with a slide hammer and the special tool. Its a thin rod that goes through the pin. Once through you thread a small sleeve onto the end of it and bang them out. Takes about ten seconds. Its a Kent-Moore tool #J-36620. Or you could make one once you understand how it works. It takes me less than 5 minutes to remove and replace the pads on any corner. (I disconnected the PITA e-brakes a loooong time ago  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)<P>PJ<BR>ITA #66 Black<BR>  ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Raydar||06-06-2001||07:49 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mrfiero:<BR><B>You DON'T have to remove the roll pins!!!  Don't waste your time with it......the pads will come off without removing them.  The '88 calipers are 2-piece.......the main caliper body and the outer part.  All you need to do is remove the 2 bolts that hold the outer part to the main body and pop it off.  You may need to use a screwdriver to pry it off of the rollpins, but it will come off.  Next, remove the outboard pad and remove the rotor (you need to resurface it anyways).  Now you can remove the inbaord pad.  That's it!  I have never removed the rollpins on any of my '88 Fieros (and I've owned about 15).<P>Good luck.<P>  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>DOH!!!<P>I don't believe I didn't notice this, when I took mine apart. <BR>Would have saved me a bunch of time.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><BR>I don't think there's any harm in doing it this way. When I bought my replacement rear calipers, they didn't have the bridge (front half), anyway. Had to transfer all the bits and pieces from my old calipers. <BR>Yeesh.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 06-06-2001).]||||reg||1
